  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: A trough will be deepening along the \ncoast as a Low drops south off the coast of California. Models \nhave been fairly consistent with the overall pattern. The main \ndifference is the Low is now expected to farther distance off \nthe coast. While the Inland Northwest was not expected to \nreceive any precip from it, the distance limits the cloud cover \nand amounts mainly high, thin clouds. The Low becomes cutoff and\na split flow pattern develops over the Northwest. Dry, warm \nconditions are expected over the Inland Northwest through most \nof Wednesday. Precip chances across the region remain near 0 \npercent through Wednesday. The region can expect warm daytime \nand cold overnight conditions. Winds will be generally calm \nexcept a brief period of breezy winds down the Okanogan Valley \non Tuesday with gusts into the upper 20 mph range. Sunday highs \nwill be 50s and low 60s and slightly increase through midweek \ninto the 60s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. \nOvernight patchy frost could impact any remaining gardens and \nsensitive seasonal plants. \n\nLate Wednesday through Sunday: The Low finally ejects northeast\ntoward Montana as a trough moves into the Pacific Northwest \novernight Wednesday into Thursday. A wrap around band of \nmoisture will bring increased shower chances to Southeast WA and\nthe Idaho Panhandle on Thursday with a 40-60 percent \nprobability of at least a tenth of an inch. A trough pattern \nwill be over the Pacific Northwest behind the exiting Low. It \nwill keep the Inland Northwest under a cool, wet conditions \nthrough the weekend with a Low tracking through North \nWashington. Friday will have mountain showers with breezy winds \nacross the Basin. Wind speeds will be teens with gust into the \nupper 20s mph range. Through the weekend, low land locations \nhave 25-75 percent probability of receiving a few hundredths to \n0.2 inches of precip. Cascades should see high amount range of \n0.1-0.5 of an inch. Snow levels dropping down to around 4000 \nfeet through the weekend. Back country trekkers should prepare \nfor cold, light snow conditions. Highs will be in the 50s to low\n60s. Overnight lows will be in the upper 20s and 30s. /JDC",
